                                                                                                                                                            <h2 class="ielts-reading-question-section-heading">
                                            Questions 32-36                                                                                                                                                
                                                                                                                                    </h2>
                                        <div class="ielts-reading-question-section-content">
                                            <div class="ielts-reading-section-content-wrapper"><p>Complete the summary using the list of words, <strong>A-H</strong>, below.</p>
<p><em>Write the correct letter, <strong>A-H</strong>, in boxes on your answer sheet.</em></p>
<p class="ielts-reading-passage-subhead"><strong><strong>The benefits of humour</strong></strong></p>
<p>In one study at Australian National University, randomly chosen groups of participants were shown one of three videos, each designed to generate a different kind of <span class="ielts-reading-question-item"><span class="ielts-inline-select-wrap"><strong class="ielts-q-badge">32</strong><select data-qnum="32" name="question_32" class="ielts-inline-select"><option value="">[ 32 ] Tanlang...</option><option value="A. laughter">A. laughter</option><option value="B. relaxing">B. relaxing</option><option value="C. boring">C. boring</option><option value="D. anxiety">D. anxiety</option><option value="E. stimulating">E. stimulating</option><option value="F. emotion">F. emotion</option><option value="G. enjoyment">G. enjoyment</option><option value="H. amusing">H. amusing</option></select></span></span>. When all participants were then given a deliberately frustrating task to do, it was found that those who had watched the <span class="ielts-reading-question-item"><span class="ielts-inline-select-wrap"><strong class="ielts-q-badge">33</strong><select data-qnum="33" name="question_33" class="ielts-inline-select"><option value="">[ 33 ] Tanlang...</option><option value="A. laughter">A. laughter</option><option value="B. relaxing">B. relaxing</option><option value="C. boring">C. boring</option><option value="D. anxiety">D. anxiety</option><option value="E. stimulating">E. stimulating</option><option value="F. emotion">F. emotion</option><option value="G. enjoyment">G. enjoyment</option><option value="H. amusing">H. amusing</option></select></span></span> video persisted with the task for longer and tried harder to accomplish the task than either of the other two groups.</p>
<p>A second study in which participants were asked to perform a particularly <span class="ielts-reading-question-item"><span class="ielts-inline-select-wrap"><strong class="ielts-q-badge">34</strong><select data-qnum="34" name="question_34" class="ielts-inline-select"><option value="">[ 34 ] Tanlang...</option><option value="A. laughter">A. laughter</option><option value="B. relaxing">B. relaxing</option><option value="C. boring">C. boring</option><option value="D. anxiety">D. anxiety</option><option value="E. stimulating">E. stimulating</option><option value="F. emotion">F. emotion</option><option value="G. enjoyment">G. enjoyment</option><option value="H. amusing">H. amusing</option></select></span></span> task produced similar results. According to researchers David Cheng and Lu Wang, these findings suggest that humour not only reduces <span class="ielts-reading-question-item"><span class="ielts-inline-select-wrap"><strong class="ielts-q-badge">35</strong><select data-qnum="35" name="question_35" class="ielts-inline-select"><option value="">[ 35 ] Tanlang...</option><option value="A. laughter">A. laughter</option><option value="B. relaxing">B. relaxing</option><option value="C. boring">C. boring</option><option value="D. anxiety">D. anxiety</option><option value="E. stimulating">E. stimulating</option><option value="F. emotion">F. emotion</option><option value="G. enjoyment">G. enjoyment</option><option value="H. amusing">H. amusing</option></select></span></span> and helps build social connections but it may also have a <span class="ielts-reading-question-item"><span class="ielts-inline-select-wrap"><strong class="ielts-q-badge">36</strong><select data-qnum="36" name="question_36" class="ielts-inline-select"><option value="">[ 36 ] Tanlang...</option><option value="A. laughter">A. laughter</option><option value="B. relaxing">B. relaxing</option><option value="C. boring">C. boring</option><option value="D. anxiety">D. anxiety</option><option value="E. stimulating">E. stimulating</option><option value="F. emotion">F. emotion</option><option value="G. enjoyment">G. enjoyment</option><option value="H. amusing">H. amusing</option></select></span></span> Effect on the body and mind.</p>
</div>                                                <div class="options-dnd-panel dnd-panel dnd-panel--inline" data-dnd-group="9226">
